= Distributional semantics =

== Overview ==
'''Distributional semantics''' is a family of approaches that model semantic behavior from patterns in the linguistic contexts in which words, phrases, sentences, or other units occur.

The basic intuition is distributional: expressions that occur in similar contexts often have related semantic behavior.

Distributional methods are central to computational linguistics, information retrieval, and modern language modeling, but distributional evidence is not identical to a complete theory of meaning.

== Distributional hypothesis ==
A classic distributional idea is that linguistic items can be characterized partly by the environments in which they occur.

If two words repeatedly appear in similar contexts, a model can represent them as close in a derived semantic space.

This supports useful generalization without requiring every semantic feature to be hand-coded.

== Count-based models ==
Early and classical models build high-dimensional vectors from co-occurrence statistics.

A simplified term–context matrix can be written:

<pre>
Xᵢⱼ = association(termᵢ, contextⱼ)
</pre>

Association may be raw frequency, pointwise mutual information, TF–IDF-like weighting, or another statistic.

Dimensionality reduction can compress the matrix into a lower-dimensional space.

== Learned embeddings ==
Neural methods learn dense vectors that predict words or contexts.

A word embedding assigns a vector:

<pre>
w → v_w ∈ ℝᵈ
</pre>

Geometric relations among vectors can support [[Semantic similarity|similarity]] search, analogy-like behavior, clustering, and downstream prediction.

== Contextual representations ==
Static embeddings assign one main vector to a word type.

Contextualized language models instead compute token representations conditioned on surrounding text, so the representation of a polysemous word can change across uses.

This improves many tasks but does not imply that every contextual vector corresponds to one discrete human-interpretable sense.

== What distributional models capture ==
Distributional models can encode useful information about:

* topical association;
* syntactic behavior;
* lexical similarity;
* selectional preferences;
* semantic fields;
* contextual word usage;
* sentence or document similarity.

The exact information depends on training data, objective, architecture, tokenization, and representation layer.

== Limits ==
Distributional co-occurrence does not by itself establish:

* real-world [[Reference|reference]];
* truth conditions;
* causal knowledge;
* logical entailment;
* conceptual identity;
* human-like understanding.

Words can occur in similar contexts for reasons that reflect association rather than semantic similarity.

Models can also reproduce corpus bias, historical contingencies, and annotation artifacts.

== Relation to lexical semantics ==
[[Lexical semantics]] studies word meaning using linguistic and theoretical distinctions.

Distributional semantics provides empirical/computational representations based on usage patterns.

The two can inform each other, but:

<pre>
distributional similarity ≠ lexical-semantic identity
</pre>

